    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d188">

                <pb facs="0436" n="404" xml:id="pg404"/>

                <head>188. Memorandum From the Chief of Naval Operations (Burke) to the Director of
                    the Joint Staff (Wheeler)<anchor n="0" xml:id="d188-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1961-04-29">April 29,
                    1961</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJ</label>
                    <item>Landing in Cuba on short notice</item>
                </list>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Yesterday the Secretary of Defense was interested in what we could do in
                        Cuba on five days notice. We were giving him a horseback estimate which is
                        not the best way to do it.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Have the Joint Staff and/or CINCLANT come up with an outline plan on what
                        we could get into Cuba five days after the President says go, with an
                        indication of what additional forces we could get if we had three more days.
                        This plan should also indicate how fast we could build up our strength after
                        D-Day.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Arleigh Burke</hi><anchor n="1"
                            xml:id="d188-f2"/></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
